PuzzleBank.setSearch("en", {
  "geo-ambiguous-ssa": {"t":"Two triangles, one set of data","s":"In triangle $ABC$, $a = \\sqrt3$, $b = \\sqrt2$ and $B = 45^\\circ$ (side $a$ is opposite $A$, side $b$ opposite $B$). <b>Which of the following can be the angle $A$?</b>","k":"In triangle ABC , a = 3 , b = 2 and B = 45° (side a is opposite A , side b opposite B ). Which of the following can be the angle A ? Side BC = a = √3 is horizontal with B on the left. A ray from B makes 45° with BC. A circle around C with radius b = √2 cuts the ray in two points, giving two possible positions A1 and A2 of vertex A. The law of sines gives sin A — and a sine value belongs to two angles. Law of sines Two candidate angles Are both possible? The two triangles Check every option"},
  "geo-centroid-area": {"t":"The centroid’s third","s":"Triangle $ABC$ has area $36$ and $G$ is its centroid (where the medians meet). <b>Which of the following equal the area of $\\triangle GBC$?</b>","k":"Triangle ABC has area 36 and G is its centroid (where the medians meet). Which of the following equal the area of GBC ? Triangle ABC with its three medians meeting at the centroid G; the triangle GBC on the base BC is shaded. G lies two thirds of the way down the median from A , so its height above BC is one third of the height of A . The centroid divides medians 2 : 1 Same base, a third of the height Check every option"},
  "geo-chord-inscribed-angle": {"t":"A chord of length 8","s":"Circle $O$ has radius $5$ and chord $AB = 8$. Point $C$ lies on the major arc $AB$. <b>Which of the following are true?</b>","k":"Circle O has radius 5 and chord AB = 8 . Point C lies on the major arc AB . Which of the following are true? A circle with centre O and radius 5. A horizontal chord AB of length 8 lies below the centre. C is a point on the larger arc above, forming the inscribed angle ACB. Drop the perpendicular from O to AB : it bisects the chord. The perpendicular from the centre Central angle Inscribed angle Area of AOB Check every option"},
  "geo-cone-inscribed-sphere": {"t":"A ball inside a cone","s":"A right circular cone has base radius $3$ and height $4$. A sphere touches its base and its lateral surface. <b>Which of the following equal the radius $r$ of the sphere?</b>","k":"A right circular cone has base radius 3 and height 4 . A sphere touches its base and its lateral surface. Which of the following equal the radius r of the sphere? The cross-section through the axis: an isosceles triangle with base 6, height 4 and slant sides 5, with the circle of the sphere inscribed in it. Cut through the axis: the sphere becomes the incircle of a 5 - 5 - 6 triangle, and r = area semiperimeter . Cut through the axis Inradius = area ÷ semiperimeter Check every option"},
  "geo-cube-diagonal": {"t":"The diagonal of the cube","s":"Cube $ABCD\\text{-}A_1B_1C_1D_1$ has edge $2$. Let $\\theta$ be the angle between the space diagonal $AC_1$ and the base $ABCD$. <b>Which of the following are true?</b>","k":"Cube ABCD - A 1B 1C 1D 1 has edge 2 . Let be the angle between the space diagonal AC 1 and the base ABCD . Which of the following are true? A cube drawn in oblique view: base ABCD at the bottom (A front-left, B front-right, C back-right, D back-left) and top A1B1C1D1. The space diagonal goes from A to C1. Edge length 2. The projection of AC 1 onto the base is the face diagonal AC . The face diagonal The space diagonal The angle with the base Surface and volume Check every option"},
  "geo-ellipse-focal-60": {"t":"The focal triangle of an ellipse","s":"For the ellipse $\\dfrac{x^2}{25} + \\dfrac{y^2}{9} = 1$ with foci $F_1, F_2$, a point $P$ on the ellipse satisfies $\\angle F_1PF_2 = 60^\\circ$. <b>Which of the following are true?</b>","k":"For the ellipse x 2 25 + y 2 9 = 1 with foci F 1, F 2 , a point P on the ellipse satisfies F 1PF 2 = 60° . Which of the following are true? The ellipse x²/25 + y²/9 = 1 centred at the origin, foci F1(−4, 0) and F2(4, 0), and a point P on the ellipse in the first quadrant with angle F1PF2 = 60°. Use |PF 1| + |PF 2| = 2a together with the law of cosines in F 1PF 2 . Read off a , b , c Definition of the ellipse Law of cosines at P Area Check every option"},
  "geo-incircle-6-8-10": {"t":"The circle inside the right triangle","s":"Triangle $ABC$ has a right angle at $C$, with $AC = 6$ and $BC = 8$. A circle with centre $I$ is inscribed in the triangle, touching all three sides. <b>Which of the following equal the radius $r$ of the circle?</b>","k":"Triangle ABC has a right angle at C , with AC = 6 and BC = 8 . A circle with centre I is inscribed in the triangle, touching all three sides. Which of the following equal the radius r of the circle? Right triangle ABC with the right angle at C. Leg AC = 6 is vertical, leg BC = 8 is horizontal, the hypotenuse AB is not given. A circle with centre I is inscribed in the triangle (tangent to all three sides); its radius r is marked from I down to side BC. Join I to the three vertices: the triangle splits into three triangles whose heights are all r . Find the hypotenuse Area of the triangle Cut the triangle at the centre I Semiperimeter Solve for r Cross-check with tangent lengths Check every option"},
  "geo-law-cosines-120": {"t":"An obtuse angle of 120°","s":"In triangle $ABC$, $AB = 3$, $AC = 5$ and $\\angle A = 120^\\circ$. <b>Which of the following statements are true?</b>","k":"In triangle ABC , AB = 3 , AC = 5 and A = 120° . Which of the following statements are true? Triangle ABC with AB = 3 along the bottom, AC = 5, and the obtuse angle A = 120° between them; side BC is unknown. Use the law of cosines for BC ; remember that cos 120° = - 12 . Law of cosines Area The other angles (law of sines) Circumradius and perimeter Check every option"},
  "geo-lens-two-quarters": {"t":"The leaf inside the square","s":"In square $ABCD$ with side $4$, two quarter circles of radius $4$ are drawn: one centred at $A$ and one centred at $C$, both from $B$ to $D$. <b>Which of the following equal the area of the shaded “leaf” between them?</b>","k":"In square ABCD with side 4 , two quarter circles of radius 4 are drawn: one centred at A and one centred at C , both from B to D . Which of the following equal the area of the shaded “leaf” between them? Square ABCD of side 4 (A bottom-left, C top-right). A quarter circle centred at A goes from B to D, another centred at C also from B to D; the leaf-shaped region between the two arcs is shaded. The leaf is two circular segments cut off by the diagonal BD . One quarter circle Remove the triangle ABD Two segments Check with inclusion–exclusion Check every option"},
  "geo-parabola-focal-chord": {"t":"A chord through the focus","s":"A line through the focus $F$ of the parabola $y^2 = 4x$ with slope $1$ meets the parabola at $A$ and $B$. <b>What is $|AB|$?</b>","k":"A line through the focus F of the parabola y 2 = 4x with slope 1 meets the parabola at A and B . What is |AB| ? The parabola y² = 4x opening to the right, focus F(1, 0), directrix x = −1. A line of slope 1 through F cuts the parabola at A (upper right) and B (near the vertex). For a chord through the focus, |AB| = x 1 + x 2 + p (distance to the directrix). Focus and the line Intersect Distance to the directrix Check with the angle formula Check every option"},
  "geo-point-line-distance": {"t":"How far from the line?","s":"<b>What is the distance from the point $P(1, 2)$ to the line $3x + 4y - 1 = 0$?</b>","k":"What is the distance from the point P(1, 2) to the line 3x + 4y - 1 = 0 ? Coordinate axes with the line 3x + 4y − 1 = 0, the point P(1, 2) above it and the perpendicular from P to the line. d = |a x 0 + b y 0 + c| sqrt a 2 + b 2 . Use the distance formula Check with the foot of the perpendicular Check every option"},
  "geo-regular-tetrahedron": {"t":"The regular tetrahedron and its sphere","s":"A regular tetrahedron $ABCD$ has all edges equal to $2$. <b>Which of the following are true?</b> ($V$ is its volume and $R$ the radius of its circumscribed sphere.)","k":"A regular tetrahedron ABCD has all edges equal to 2 . Which of the following are true? ( V is its volume and R the radius of its circumscribed sphere.) A regular tetrahedron with apex A above the triangular base BCD (D is at the back, its edges drawn dashed). O is the centre of the base; the height AO is dashed. The centre of the circumscribed sphere lies on AO. The foot O of the height is the centroid of the base, with BO = 2 3 3 . Base area and centroid Height Volume Circumscribed sphere Check every option"},
  "geo-similar-parallel": {"t":"Cut by a parallel line","s":"In triangle $ABC$, $D$ is on $AB$ and $E$ is on $AC$ with $DE \\parallel BC$ and $AD : DB = 2 : 3$. The area of $\\triangle ADE$ is $8$. <b>What is the area of $\\triangle ABC$?</b>","k":"In triangle ABC , D is on AB and E is on AC with DE BC and AD : DB = 2 : 3 . The area of ADE is 8 . What is the area of ABC ? Triangle ABC with apex A at the top and base BC at the bottom. A segment DE parallel to BC cuts the sides AB and AC, with AD : DB = 2 : 3. The small top triangle ADE has area 8. Similar triangles: areas scale with the square of the ratio of sides. Similar triangles Areas scale with the square The trapezoid Check every option"},
  "geo-vector-dot-square": {"t":"A dot product in the square","s":"Square $ABCD$ has side $2$, and $E$ is the midpoint of $CD$. <b>What is $\\overrightarrow{AE}\\cdot\\overrightarrow{BD}$?</b>","k":"Square ABCD has side 2 , and E is the midpoint of CD . What is AE · BD ? Square ABCD with side 2: A bottom-left, B bottom-right, C top-right, D top-left. E is the midpoint of the top side CD. Vectors AE and BD are drawn as arrows. Put A at the origin, B = (2,0) , D = (0,2) . Coordinates The two vectors Dot product Without coordinates Check every option"},
  "trig-circumradius-law-sines": {"t":"The circle around a triangle","s":"In triangle $ABC$, side $a = BC = 6$ and $\\angle A = 30^\\circ$. <b>Which of the following equal the circumradius $R$?</b>","k":"In triangle ABC , side a = BC = 6 and A = 30° . Which of the following equal the circumradius R ? A triangle ABC inscribed in its circumcircle with centre O; the chord BC has length 6 and the angle at A is 30°. a sin A = 2R . Extended law of sines See it with the central angle Check every option"},
  "trig-equation-count": {"t":"How many crossings?","s":"<b>How many solutions does $\\sin 2x = \\cos x$ have in $[0, 2\\pi)$?</b>","k":"How many solutions does sin 2x = cos x have in [0, 2 ) ? The graphs of y = sin 2x and y = cos x on [0, 2π); they cross four times. sin 2x = 2 sin x cos x . Move everything to one side and factor — do not divide by cos x . Factor Solve each factor Check every option"},
  "trig-max-a-sin-b-cos": {"t":"The highest wave","s":"<b>Which of the following equal the maximum value of $f(x) = 3\\sin x + 4\\cos x$?</b>","k":"Which of the following equal the maximum value of f(x) = 3 sin x + 4 cos x ? The graph of 3 sin x + 4 cos x over one period: a sine wave of height 5, with dashed lines at y = 5 and y = −5. Write 3 sin x + 4 cos x = R sin (x + ) with R = sqrt 3 2 + 4 2 . Combine into one sine Read off the maximum Check every option"},
  "trig-sinusoid-properties": {"t":"Reading a sinusoid","s":"Let $f(x) = 2\\sin\\!\\left(2x + \\tfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)$. <b>Which of the following are true?</b>","k":"Let f(x) = 2 sin \\! (2x + 3 ) . Which of the following are true? The graph of 2 sin(2x + π/3) over two periods; its first maximum is at x = π/12. For A sin ( x + ) : period 2 , amplitude A , shift - . Period and amplitude Phase shift and maximum Check every option"},
  "trig-tan-ratio": {"t":"Only the tangent is known","s":"If $\\tan\\theta = 2$, <b>what is $\\dfrac{\\sin\\theta + \\cos\\theta}{\\sin\\theta - \\cos\\theta}$?</b>","k":"If tan = 2 , what is sin + cos sin - cos ? A right triangle with legs 1 and 2 and hypotenuse √5; the angle θ is opposite the leg 2, so tan θ = 2. Divide numerator and denominator by cos . Divide by cos θ Check with the triangle Check every option"},
  "trig-tower-30-60": {"t":"The tower seen twice","s":"From a point $P$ on flat ground, the top $T$ of a vertical tower is seen at an angle of elevation of $30^\\circ$. After walking $20$ m straight towards the tower, to $Q$, the angle of elevation is $60^\\circ$. <b>Which of the following equal the height $h$ of the tower (in metres)?</b>","k":"From a point P on flat ground, the top T of a vertical tower is seen at an angle of elevation of 30° . After walking 20 m straight towards the tower, to Q , the angle of elevation is 60° . Which of the following equal the height h of the tower (in metres)? Flat ground with points P, Q and the foot F of the tower, in that order. PQ = 20 m, QF = x is unknown. The tower FT of height h is vertical. Angle of elevation of T from P is 30°, from Q is 60°. Look at the angles of triangle PQT — two of them are equal. Angles of triangle PQT Two equal angles → two equal sides The right triangle QFT Cross-check with two tangents Check every option"},
  "alg-absolute-inequality": {"t":"Closer than five","s":"<b>Which of the following describe all real $x$ with $|2x - 3| < 5$?</b>","k":"Which of the following describe all real x with $|2x - 3| A number line with the open interval from −1 to 4 shaded; 3/2, the midpoint, is marked. |u| < 5 -5 < u < 5 . Remove the absolute value Isolate x Check every option"},
  "alg-arithmetic-sum": {"t":"From two terms to a sum","s":"In an arithmetic sequence $a_3 = 7$ and $a_7 = 19$. <b>What is the sum of the first ten terms, $S_{10}$?</b>","k":"In an arithmetic sequence a 3 = 7 and a 7 = 19 . What is the sum of the first ten terms, S 10 ? Dots for the terms a₁ … a₁₀ lying on a straight line; a₃ = 7 and a₇ = 19 are highlighted. a 7 - a 3 = 4d . Difference and first term Sum = average × number of terms Check every option"},
  "alg-geometric-sum": {"t":"Tripling five times","s":"A geometric sequence has $a_1 = 2$ and ratio $r = 3$. <b>What is the sum of its first five terms, $S_5$?</b>","k":"A geometric sequence has a 1 = 2 and ratio r = 3 . What is the sum of its first five terms, S 5 ? A bar chart of the first five terms 2, 6, 18, 54, 162, each bar three times taller than the previous one. S n = a 1 r n - 1 r - 1 . Use the formula Check by adding Check every option"},
  "alg-log-equation": {"t":"Two logarithms make eight","s":"<b>Which of the following are solutions of $\\log_2 x + \\log_2(x - 2) = 3$?</b>","k":"Which of the following are solutions of 2 x + 2(x - 2) = 3 ? The graph of y = log₂x + log₂(x − 2), defined only for x > 2, crossing the line y = 3 once, at x = 4. 2 a + 2 b = 2(ab) , and both x and x - 2 must be positive. Combine the logarithms Solve and check the domain Check every option"},
  "alg-rectangle-area-40": {"t":"Three metres longer","s":"The length of a rectangle is $3$ more than its width $w$, and its area is $40$. <b>Which of the following are equal to $w$?</b>","k":"The length of a rectangle is 3 more than its width w , and its area is 40 . Which of the following are equal to w ? A rectangle with width w (short side) and length w + 3 (long side); the area inside is 40. Write the area as w(w+3) and solve the quadratic equation. Turn the words into an equation Factor Keep the root that makes sense Same answer with the quadratic formula Check every option"},
  "alg-vieta-sum-squares": {"t":"Squares of the roots","s":"$\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are the roots of $x^2 - 5x + 3 = 0$. <b>Which of the following equal $\\alpha^2 + \\beta^2$?</b>","k":"and are the roots of x 2 - 5x + 3 = 0 . Which of the following equal 2 + 2 ? The parabola y = x² − 5x + 3 crossing the x-axis at α ≈ 0.70 and β ≈ 4.30. Vieta: + = 5 and = 3 . Vieta’s formulas Complete the square Check every option"},
  "mat-cayley-hamilton": {"t":"A matrix obeys its own equation","s":"$A$ is a $2\\times2$ matrix with $\\operatorname{tr}A = 5$ and $\\det A = 6$, and $I$ is the identity. <b>Which of the following must be true?</b>","k":"A is a 2 2 matrix with tr A = 5 and A = 6 , and I is the identity. Which of the following must be true? The graph of the characteristic polynomial λ² − 5λ + 6, crossing the axis at the eigenvalues 2 and 3. The characteristic polynomial is 2 - 5 + 6 , and A itself satisfies it. The characteristic polynomial Cayley–Hamilton Check every option"},
  "mat-cramer-2x2": {"t":"Cramer’s rule","s":"Solve $\\begin{cases} 2x + 3y = 8 \\\\ x - y = -1 \\end{cases}$ with determinants. Let $D$ be the determinant of the coefficient matrix. <b>Which of the following are true?</b>","k":"Solve cases 2x + 3y = 8 \\\\ x - y = -1 cases with determinants. Let D be the determinant of the coefficient matrix. Which of the following are true? Coordinate axes with the lines 2x + 3y = 8 and x − y = −1; they cross at the point (1, 2). The coefficient matrix (2 3; 1 −1) is written in a corner. x = D x D and y = D y D , where D x ( D y ) is D with the x -column ( y -column) replaced by the constants 8, -1 . Write the system as a matrix Replace the x-column Replace the y-column Check on the graph Check every option"},
  "mat-det-3x3-cofactor": {"t":"A 3×3 determinant","s":"Let $A = \\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 6 \\end{pmatrix}$. <b>Which of the following is true?</b>","k":"Let A = pmatrix 2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 6 pmatrix . Which of the following is true? The 3×3 matrix A with rows (2, 1, 3), (0, 4, 5) and (1, 0, 6), written between brackets. Expand along the first column: it contains a 0 , so only two 2 2 minors are needed. Expand along the first column The two minors Check with the rule of Sarrus Check every option"},
  "mat-det-parallelogram-area": {"t":"The area hidden in a determinant","s":"The vectors $\\vec u = (3, 1)$ and $\\vec v = (1, 2)$ span a parallelogram. <b>Which of the following equal its area?</b>","k":"The vectors u = (3, 1) and v = (1, 2) span a parallelogram. Which of the following equal its area? A unit grid. From the origin O the vector u goes to (3, 1) and the vector v goes to (1, 2); the parallelogram O, (3, 1), (4, 3), (1, 2) is shaded. Put u and v as the columns of a 2 2 matrix: the area is the absolute value of its determinant. Vectors as columns Compute the determinant Check with the box method Check every option"},
  "mat-det-properties": {"t":"Scaling a 3×3 matrix","s":"$A$ is a $3\\times3$ matrix with $\\det A = 2$. <b>Which of the following are true?</b>","k":"A is a 3 3 matrix with A = 2 . Which of the following are true? A small cube of volume V and a cube three times as wide, of volume 27V: scaling all three directions by 3 multiplies volumes — and 3×3 determinants — by 3³ = 27. Multiplying one row by 3 multiplies the determinant by 3 . How many rows does 3A multiply? One row at a time The inverse The other rules Check every option"},
  "mat-eigenvalues-2x2": {"t":"Directions that do not turn","s":"Let $A = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 \\end{pmatrix}$. <b>Which of the following are eigenvalues of $A$?</b>","k":"Let A = pmatrix 4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3 pmatrix . Which of the following are eigenvalues of A ? Coordinate axes. The vector v = (1, 1) and its image Av = (5, 5) lie on the same line; the vector w = (1, −2) and its image Aw = (2, −4) also lie on one line. Solve (A - I) = 0 , a quadratic equation in . The characteristic equation Solve the quadratic See them on the picture Check with trace and determinant Check every option"},
  "mat-inverse-2x2": {"t":"Undo the matrix","s":"Let $A = \\begin{pmatrix} 4 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 6 \\end{pmatrix}$. <b>Which of the following equal $A^{-1}$?</b>","k":"Let A = pmatrix 4 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 6 pmatrix . Which of the following equal A -1 ? The matrix A = (4 7; 2 6). Arrows show the recipe for the inverse: swap 4 and 6 on the main diagonal and change the signs of 7 and 2. For pmatrix a & b \\\\ c & d pmatrix the inverse is 1 ad - bc pmatrix d & -b \\\\ -c & a pmatrix . Is it invertible? Swap, change signs, divide Check: the product is the identity Check every option"},
  "mat-no-solution-parameter": {"t":"Parallel lines never meet","s":"<b>For which value of $k$ does the system $\\begin{cases} x + y = 2 \\\\ 2x + ky = 5 \\end{cases}$ have no solution?</b>","k":"For which value of k does the system cases x + y = 2 \\\\ 2x + ky = 5 cases have no solution? The lines x + y = 2 and 2x + 2y = 5 (the case k = 2): two parallel lines that never meet. No unique solution needs pmatrix 1&1\\\\2&k pmatrix = 0 ; then check the right-hand sides. Determinant zero Contradiction Check every option"},
  "mat-product-order": {"t":"Order matters","s":"Let $A = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}$ and $B = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}$. <b>Which of the following are true?</b>","k":"Let A = pmatrix 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 pmatrix and B = pmatrix 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 pmatrix . Which of the following are true? The matrices A = (1 2; 0 1) and B = (1 0; 3 1) written side by side, ready to be multiplied row by column. Entry (i, j) of AB is row i of A times column j of B . Then do BA the same way and compare. Multiply row by column Now the other order Determinants agree Check every option"},
  "mat-rotation-cube": {"t":"Three turns of 60°","s":"$R = \\begin{pmatrix} \\cos 60^\\circ & -\\sin 60^\\circ \\\\ \\sin 60^\\circ & \\cos 60^\\circ \\end{pmatrix}$ rotates the plane by $60^\\circ$, and $I$ is the identity matrix. <b>Which of the following equal $R^3$?</b>","k":"R = pmatrix cos 60° & - sin 60° \\\\ sin 60° & cos 60° pmatrix rotates the plane by 60° , and I is the identity matrix. Which of the following equal R 3 ? A circle around the origin. The point P = (1, 0) is rotated three times by 60°: to RP at 60°, R²P at 120° and R³P at 180°, which is −P. Doing a rotation three times rotates by three times the angle. Write out R Rotations add up Three turns: a half turn Check by multiplying Check every option"},
  "mat-shear-power": {"t":"Ten slides of a shear","s":"Let $A = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}$. <b>Which of the following equals $A^{10}$?</b>","k":"Let A = pmatrix 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 pmatrix . Which of the following equals A 10 ? A square and its images under A and A²: parallelograms with the same base whose top edge slides one side length further to the right each time. Compute A 2 and A 3 , look for the pattern, then jump to the 10th power. The first powers The pattern Ten slides Check every option"},
  "mat-singular-parameter": {"t":"When does the inverse disappear?","s":"For which values of $k$ is $M = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & k & 6 \\\\ 1 & 1 & k \\end{pmatrix}$ <b>not</b> invertible? <b>Which of the following are such values?</b>","k":"For which values of k is M = pmatrix 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & k & 6 \\\\ 1 & 1 & k pmatrix not invertible? Which of the following are such values? The 3×3 matrix M with rows (1, 2, 3), (2, k, 6) and (1, 1, k); the two entries k are highlighted. A square matrix is not invertible exactly when its determinant is 0 . Expand along the first row. Not invertible means determinant zero Expand along the first row Find the roots See the dependent rows Check every option"},
  "cx-argument-quadrant": {"t":"Which way does it point?","s":"<b>Which of the following are an argument of $z = -1 + \\sqrt3\\, i$?</b> (Angles measured from the positive real axis.)","k":"Which of the following are an argument of z = -1 + 3\\, i ? (Angles measured from the positive real axis.) The complex plane with z = −1 + √3 i in the second quadrant, at distance 2 from the origin; the angle from the positive real axis is marked. Draw it: the real part is negative and the imaginary part positive, so the angle is between 90° and 180° . Find the quadrant Modulus and angle Why the calculator is wrong Check every option"},
  "cx-conjugate-facts": {"t":"A number and its mirror image","s":"Let $z = 2 - 3i$ and let $\\bar z$ be its conjugate. <b>Which of the following are true?</b>","k":"Let z = 2 - 3i and let z be its conjugate. Which of the following are true? The complex plane with z = 2 − 3i and its conjugate 2 + 3i, mirror images in the real axis. z = 2 + 3i : only the sign of the imaginary part changes. The conjugate Sum and product Other quantities Check every option"},
  "cx-cube-roots-of-unity": {"t":"A cube root of one","s":"$\\omega$ is a non-real cube root of $1$ (so $\\omega^3 = 1$, $\\omega \\ne 1$). <b>Which of the following equal $(1 + \\omega)(1 + \\omega^2)$?</b>","k":"is a non-real cube root of 1 (so 3 = 1 , 1 ). Which of the following equal (1 + )(1 + 2) ? The unit circle with the three cube roots of unity 1, ω and ω² at 0°, 120° and 240°, forming an equilateral triangle. Expand and use 1 + + 2 = 0 and 3 = 1 . Two facts about ω Expand Check every option"},
  "cx-de-moivre-power": {"t":"A spiral of powers","s":"<b>Which of the following equal $(1 + i)^8$?</b>","k":"Which of the following equal (1 + i) 8 ? The complex plane with 1 + i and its powers (1 + i)² = 2i, (1 + i)³ = −2 + 2i and (1 + i)⁴ = −4: each step turns 45° and stretches by √2. Write 1 + i in polar form: modulus 2 , argument 45° . Raising to a power multiplies the angle. Polar form De Moivre’s formula Check with squares Check every option"},
  "cx-division-conjugate": {"t":"Dividing by a complex number","s":"<b>Which of the following equals $\\dfrac{5 + 5i}{1 + 2i}$?</b>","k":"Which of the following equals 5 + 5i 1 + 2i ? The complex plane with 5 + 5i, 1 + 2i and the quotient 3 − i, each joined to the origin. Multiply numerator and denominator by 1 - 2i : the denominator becomes the real number 1 2 + 2 2 . Multiply by the conjugate Expand the numerator Check by multiplying back Check every option"},
  "cx-locus-circle": {"t":"A circle written with a modulus","s":"Let $S$ be the set of complex numbers $z$ with $|z - 3 - 4i| = 5$. <b>Which of the following are true?</b>","k":"Let S be the set of complex numbers z with |z - 3 - 4i| = 5 . Which of the following are true? The complex plane with the point C = 3 + 4i and the circle of radius 5 around it; the circle passes through the origin. |z - c| is the distance from z to c . Read the modulus as a distance In coordinates Test the points Check every option"},
  "cx-modulus-product": {"t":"The length of a product","s":"Let $z = 3 + 4i$ and $w = 1 - i$. <b>Which of the following equal $|zw|$?</b>","k":"Let z = 3 + 4i and w = 1 - i . Which of the following equal |zw| ? The complex plane with the points z = 3 + 4i, w = 1 − i and their product zw = 7 + i, each joined to the origin. Either multiply first and take the modulus, or use |zw| = |z|\\,|w| . Multiply Take the modulus Shortcut: moduli multiply Check every option"},
  "cx-power-of-i": {"t":"i to a large power","s":"<b>Which of the following equal $i^{2026}$?</b>","k":"Which of the following equal i 2026 ? The unit circle in the complex plane with the four points 1, i, −1, −i; arrows show that each multiplication by i turns a quarter turn. i 4 = 1 , so only the remainder of 2026 divided by 4 matters. The first powers Where 2026 lands in the cycle Check every option"},
  "cx-quadratic-roots": {"t":"A quadratic with no real roots","s":"<b>Which of the following are solutions of $z^2 + 2z + 5 = 0$?</b>","k":"Which of the following are solutions of z 2 + 2z + 5 = 0 ? The complex plane with the two roots −1 + 2i and −1 − 2i, mirror images of each other in the real axis, on the circle of radius √5 around the origin. Use the quadratic formula; the discriminant is negative, and sqrt -16 = 4i . The discriminant The quadratic formula Check with sum and product Check every option"},
  "cx-square-vertex": {"t":"Completing a square with i","s":"$ABCD$ is a square labelled counter-clockwise, with $A = 1 + i$ and $B = 4 + 2i$. <b>What is $C$?</b>","k":"ABCD is a square labelled counter-clockwise, with A = 1 + i and B = 4 + 2i . What is C ? The complex plane with A = 1 + i, B = 4 + 2i and the square ABCD drawn counter-clockwise; C = 3 + 5i and D = 4i. BC is AB turned 90° counter-clockwise: multiply B - A by i . The side vector Turn it by 90° Check every option"},
  "cx-z-plus-modulus": {"t":"A number plus its length","s":"The complex number $z$ satisfies $z + |z| = 2 + 8i$. <b>Which of the following are true?</b>","k":"The complex number z satisfies z + |z| = 2 + 8i . Which of the following are true? The complex plane with z = −15 + 8i at distance 17 from the origin; adding the real number 17 moves it to 2 + 8i. |z| is real, so Im \\,z = 8 . Write z = a + 8i . The imaginary part Solve for the real part Check every option"},
  "calc-area-parabola-line": {"t":"Between a line and a parabola","s":"<b>Which of the following equal the area of the region enclosed by $y = x^2$ and $y = 2x$?</b>","k":"Which of the following equal the area of the region enclosed by y = x 2 and y = 2x ? The parabola y = x² and the line y = 2x meeting at (0, 0) and (2, 4); the region between them is shaded. Find where they meet, then integrate (top − bottom). Intersections Integrate top minus bottom Check every option"},
  "calc-cubic-extrema": {"t":"A hill and a valley","s":"Let $f(x) = x^3 - 6x^2 + 9x + 1$. <b>Which of the following are true?</b>","k":"Let f(x) = x 3 - 6x 2 + 9x + 1 . Which of the following are true? The graph of f with a local maximum at (1, 5) and a local minimum at (3, 1). Factor f'(x) = 3x 2 - 12x + 9 and look at its sign. Critical points Values at the extrema Check every option"},
  "calc-limit-exp-taylor": {"t":"What is left of eˣ","s":"<b>What is $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to0}\\frac{e^x - 1 - x}{x^2}$?</b>","k":"What is x 0 e x - 1 - x x 2 ? The graph of (eˣ − 1 − x)/x² near 0, with a hole at x = 0 where the curve approaches the height 1/2. e x = 1 + x + x 2 2 + x 3 6 + Expand eˣ Divide and let x → 0 Check every option"},
  "calc-open-box": {"t":"The biggest open box","s":"From a $12\\times12$ square sheet, equal squares of side $x$ are cut from the corners and the sides are folded up into an open box. <b>Which of the following are true for the box of largest volume?</b>","k":"From a 12 12 square sheet, equal squares of side x are cut from the corners and the sides are folded up into an open box. Which of the following are true for the box of largest volume? A 12 × 12 square with four x × x squares shaded in the corners; the remaining cross folds up into a box with base (12 − 2x) × (12 − 2x) and height x. V(x) = x(12 - 2x) 2 for 0 < x < 6 ; solve V'(x) = 0 . The volume Set the derivative to zero Check every option"},
  "calc-pen-wall-40": {"t":"The biggest pen against the wall","s":"A farmer has $40$ m of fencing to build a rectangular pen against a long straight wall, so only three sides need fencing. <b>What is the largest area the pen can have?</b>","k":"A farmer has 40 m of fencing to build a rectangular pen against a long straight wall, so only three sides need fencing. What is the largest area the pen can have? A long wall along the top. A rectangular pen touches the wall; the two sides perpendicular to the wall have length x, the side parallel to the wall has length y, and 2x + y = 40 m of fence is used. Call the two sides touching the wall x ; the third side is then 40 - 2x . Build a model Where the slope is zero Check it is a maximum The largest area Without calculus Check every option"},
  "calc-tangent-cubic": {"t":"Touching the cubic","s":"<b>What is the equation of the tangent line to $y = x^3 - 3x$ at the point where $x = 2$?</b>","k":"What is the equation of the tangent line to y = x 3 - 3x at the point where x = 2 ? The cubic y = x³ − 3x with the point (2, 2) on it and the tangent line there. Slope = y'(2) ; then y - y 0 = m(x - x 0) . Point and slope Point–slope form Check every option"},
  "prob-binomial-two-of-four": {"t":"Exactly two hits","s":"A player hits the target with probability $\\tfrac13$ on each shot, independently. <b>Which of the following equal the probability of exactly $2$ hits in $4$ shots?</b>","k":"A player hits the target with probability 13 on each shot, independently. Which of the following equal the probability of exactly 2 hits in 4 shots? A bar chart of the binomial distribution for 4 shots with p = 1/3: 16/81, 32/81, 24/81, 8/81, 1/81 for 0 to 4 hits; the bar for 2 hits is highlighted. P(X = k) = n k p k (1 - p) n - k . Count the orders Multiply by the probability of one order Check every option"},
  "prob-committee-at-least-one": {"t":"At least one woman","s":"A committee of $3$ is chosen from $5$ men and $4$ women. <b>Which of the following equal the number of committees with at least one woman?</b>","k":"A committee of 3 is chosen from 5 men and 4 women. Which of the following equal the number of committees with at least one woman? Five blue dots for the men and four pink dots for the women, with three of the nine circled. Count all committees, then subtract those made only of men. All committees Remove the all-male ones Check every option"},
  "prob-expected-maximum": {"t":"The larger of two dice","s":"Two fair dice are rolled and $M$ is the larger of the two numbers. <b>Which of the following equal the expected value $E[M]$?</b>","k":"Two fair dice are rolled and M is the larger of the two numbers. Which of the following equal the expected value E[M] ? A 6 × 6 grid of outcomes coloured by the maximum: the L-shaped bands for max = 1, 2, …, 6 contain 1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11 cells. P(M = k) = 2k - 1 36 : the cells where the larger number is exactly k form an L shape. The distribution of M Average with these weights Check every option"},
  "prob-two-children-boy": {"t":"At least one boy","s":"A family has two children; each child is a boy or a girl with probability $\\tfrac12$, independently. You learn that <b>at least one is a boy</b>. <b>What is the probability that both are boys?</b>","k":"A family has two children; each child is a boy or a girl with probability 12 , independently. You learn that at least one is a boy . What is the probability that both are boys? A 2 × 2 table of the four equally likely families BB, BG, GB, GG; the GG cell is crossed out by the information. List the four equally likely families and remove the one ruled out. List the possibilities Condition on the information Check every option"},
  "prob-two-dice-prime": {"t":"Prime time with two dice","s":"Two fair six-sided dice are rolled and the two numbers are added. <b>Which of the following equal the probability that the sum is a prime number?</b>","k":"Two fair six-sided dice are rolled and the two numbers are added. Which of the following equal the probability that the sum is a prime number? A 6 × 6 table: columns are the first die (1–6), rows are the second die (1–6), each cell shows the sum. Make the 6 6 table of all sums: every cell is equally likely. All outcomes Which sums are prime? Count the favourable cells The probability Check every option"},
  "nt-divisors-360": {"t":"Counting the divisors of 360","s":"<b>Which of the following equal the number of positive divisors of $360$?</b>","k":"Which of the following equal the number of positive divisors of 360 ? A 4 × 6 table of all divisors of 360: rows are 1, 2, 4, 8 and columns are 1, 3, 9, 5, 15, 45; each cell is their product. 360 = 2 3·3 2·5 . A divisor picks an exponent for each prime. Prime factorisation Multiply the choices Check every option"},
  "nt-gcd-lcm-84-126": {"t":"Common factors","s":"<b>Which of the following are true for $84$ and $126$?</b>","k":"Which of the following are true for 84 and 126 ? The prime factorisations 84 = 2 · 2 · 3 · 7 and 126 = 2 · 3 · 3 · 7 written in two rows, with the shared factors 2, 3 and 7 lined up. 84 = 2 2·3·7 and 126 = 2·3 2·7 . Factor into primes Lowest and highest powers Check every option"},
  "nt-last-digit-7-2026": {"t":"The last digit of a giant","s":"$7^{2026}$ has more than 1700 digits. <b>Which of the following is its last digit?</b>","k":"7 2026 has more than 1700 digits. Which of the following is its last digit? A cycle diagram of the last digits of the powers of 7: 7¹ ends in 7, 7² in 9, 7³ in 3, 7⁴ in 1, then the pattern repeats. Write the last digits of 7, 7 2, 7 3, and look for a pattern. Only the last digit matters The pattern repeats every 4 Where 2026 lands in the cycle Check with congruences Check every option"},
  "nt-power-of-two-mod-7": {"t":"A huge power of two","s":"<b>What is the remainder when $2^{100}$ is divided by $7$?</b>","k":"What is the remainder when 2 100 is divided by 7 ? A cycle of the remainders of powers of 2 modulo 7: 2 → 4 → 1 → 2 …, repeating every three steps. 2 3 = 8 1 7 . Find the cycle Reduce the exponent mod 3 Check every option"}
});
